WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
Sachin Tendulkant just teamed up with OpenAI to explore everyday ways ChatGPT can help with curiosity and real life. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 180. CNBC TV 18 reports he posted on the social platform X about pushing ChatGPT past simple answers. ChatGPT is a chatbot, meaning software you talk to in plain sentences, built by OpenAI. The cricket legends said it already helped him

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.500
plan a recent family trip.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.520
Cricket is a bat and ball sport, and he is one of its biggest stars.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.120
The chatbot suggested ideas and handled the details.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.420
He has always been curious, he noticed that one question tends to light the next one.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.420
Chat GPT leans into that habit, and it still gets real tasks finished.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.960
Picture a pocket travel planner who never clocks out and never gets bored of your what-ifs.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.760
You toss at a destination, a timing snag, a what should we eat, and it keeps the thread

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.120
going.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.660
Each reply becomes a stepping stone, a dry place to stand while you ask the next thing.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.680
Artificial intelligence tools or AI tools are not only for people who write computer code

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:13.620
or work in labs.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.240
AI means software that can do tasks that used to take human thinking.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.500
These chatbots are turning into everyday helpers for hobbies, family life, and personal curiosity.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.720
If you are a student, you can start with a sport you already love, a trip or a school

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:30.320
project.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.960
The next question feels easy to ask, so you may try things you would have skipped.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.200
The partnership aims to share these fines in public, so you can follow along and try

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:40.980
the same ideas.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.160
Right now you can open chat GPT on your phone or computer.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:46.320
Type this.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.800
Help me plan a weekend activity around my favorite sport.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.840
You do not need a special account for basic use.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.680
Your eyes drink in a photo in one gulp, catching colors, objects, and the little story in the

00:01:58.680 --> 00:01:58.980
frame.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.600
An AI system has never seen that photo with eyeballs.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.680
It studied millions of pictures that were paired with written descriptions.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.240
That long study period is called training, like drilling with flashcards of photos and captions.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.240
When you upload a picture, the system does not look, it sorts.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.720
It breaks the image into patterns it already knows, like edges, shapes, and textures.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:27.860
Think of dumping a messy laundry basket into piles, sleeves, denim, bright reds.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.120
Then it matches those piles to words from a giant sticker book of labels.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.660
Step by step it builds a sentence by guessing which words usually sit together in similar

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:38.120
scenes.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.780
That is the same trick as your phone keyboard predicting the next word while you text.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.080
The more flashcards it saw in training, the smoother those guesses become.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.400
The write-up can sound rich and still miss a tiny object in the corner.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.640
It is working from patterns, not from real looking.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.980
It is a bit like describing a bedroom through a keyhole.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:02:59.780
You get the bed and the window.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.940
You might miss the sock on the floor.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.480
After a few tries, the strengths and the blind spots oath become obvious.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.400
The mystery was never a pair of robot eyes.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.640
It was matching at a huge scale.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.360
Try this on your phone.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.360
one. Pick any photo from your gallery without looking at it first. Someone on Reddit

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.960
turned this into a two-minute guessing challenge. Paste the picture into Chat GPT and ask it

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:29.100
to describe the image in detail. Then try to guess what the photo actually shows, using

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:35.300
only the AI's words. Go to the Chat GPT website on your phone or laptop, upload a random

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:40.920
picture, and type, describe this photo so I can guess what it is. Notice how detailed

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.120
it sounds, and notice what it gets wrong. Try this with music too. If you have

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.660
years of songs saved on your computer and a Spotify account, you can put both

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:55.840
lists into chat GPT. Spotify is the music streaming app where your plays get

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.700
saved. Ask it to suggest 10 new tracks every Friday that match your real

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:05.180
listening history. One person on Reddit said three of the first 10 suggestions

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:09.760
went straight into their playlist. That beat Spotify's own discovery tools for

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.980
them. It is like handing your whole music diary to someone, not just the last few songs

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:21.300
you played. Open Chat GPT, upload your Spotify listening history file, and add a list of

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.900
your downloaded songs. You can download that history from your account privacy settings.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:32.040
Then say, send me 10 song recommendations every Friday based on this data, and remember

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:37.700
my ratings for next time. Microsoft co-founder Bill Gates told EnGadget it is completely

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.380
easily irresponsible for powerful AI systems to go out without proper safeguards.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.700
Safeguards are like seat belts and speed limits for a very powerful car.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.540
He said the bigger near-term risk is bad actors, meaning people causing harm with the tools,

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.920
not the AI going rogue on its own.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.780
A high school English teacher used chat GPT on his phone to inspect a used paint sprayer

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:01.880
listed by a stranger.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.580
He found it on Facebook Marketplace, a place people sell used stuff.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.760
and someone on Reddit shared the story.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.080
The chatbot gave him priming steps

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.320
and safety checks he had never heard of,

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.420
like an expert on speaker phone in the seller's backyard.

